@ [baseline_commit] 272dfe7fffad0b048187bb6d082cca25ef3b1496

& [InputAudit] passes at source-intake grade
  + the original CTPR dictation remains byte-preserved and separately manifested
  + four Simula, stored-procedure, shared-inference, and FPGA questions or elaborations remain byte-preserved and separately manifested
  + prior canonical CTPR and its phase lock are exact inherited constraint sources
  + WS-CTPR-010 proof is exact completed implementation evidence
  + Simula correspondence is explicitly non-authoritative research evidence
  + no new prose is represented as verbatim user dictation

& [AuthorityAudit] finds one completed boundary and three unopened boundaries
  + [completed] immutable CTPR forms, normalization, pure validation, and malformed-input negative tests
  + [unopened_1] deterministic fake repository, logical Calendar, Planner, replay, tandem lanes, and bounded compiler-diff runtime
  + [unopened_2] effectless model-callable Cantor Process Procedure language, compiler, catalogue, admission, and invocation experiment
  + [unopened_3] optional FPGA lowering profile, synthesis target, circuit interface, resource envelope, and software-to-hardware correspondence proof
  + [conclusion] no current signature authorizes any unopened boundary

& [DelineationResult] requires two executable specifications and one deferred target profile
  + [CTPR_Runtime_Core] owns deterministic logical-time repository, Calendar, Planner, replay, tandem lane, join, invalidation, timeout, reentry, and bounded compiler-diff fixtures
  + [Cantor_Process_Procedure] owns process syntax or forms, typed input and output, declared state, logical events, yield and reactivation, budgets, validation, compilation, admission, catalogue identity, invocation, result, trace, and revocation
  + [FPGA_Lowering_Profile] owns only the eligibility contract and correspondence obligations for a future backend
  + [reason] combining all three under one signature would allow software runtime evidence to overclaim procedure-language or hardware readiness
  + [shared_IR_edge] both executable specifications may meet at a canonical typed process and dataflow IR only after each side defines its own identity and effect rules

& [PrimaryArchitectureHypothesis] is software-first and target-neutral
  + SOP remains the meaning, purpose, authority, and proof source language
  + Cantor Process Procedure is a Simula-inspired executable process profile rather than vintage Simula compatibility
  + canonical typed process IR is the portable execution contract
  + an interpreter, WASM, or native software backend is the first proof target
  + FPGA lowering is a later optional backend for eligible bounded kernels
  + model interaction occurs through structured stop-tool-result-resume turns
  + shared inference means explicitly named contexts exchanging typed messages around pinned SOP identities, not shared K/V cache, hidden activations, or weights

& [ProcedureAuthoritySeparation] is mandatory
  + proposal, validation, compilation, admission, signing, catalogue storage, invocation, effect mediation, and revocation are distinct operations
  + a model may author ProcedureCandidate source without gaining authority to admit, sign, persist, invoke, or widen it
  + an admitted procedure is content-addressed, version-pinned, schema-checked, budgeted, revocable, and replayable
  + direct self-modification, self-signing, self-admission, ambient I/O, undeclared model calls, unbounded event creation, and inherited ambient authority are prohibited

& [FPGAFitAssessment] is qualified
  + conventional FPGA implementation languages are VHDL, Verilog, and SystemVerilog
  + high-level synthesis can lower a restricted C or C++ algorithm into RTL
  + a dataflow or handshake IR is relevant to process queues and independent bounded stages
  + these languages are strong for circuit structure, parallel datapaths, memories, registers, and timing but weak for human-readable semantic purpose and authority negotiation
  + strongest future kernels are digest pipelines, catalogue lookup, bounded graph traversal, relation scoring, deterministic arbitration, FIFO transport, barrier accounting, and trace emission
  + model generation, semantic judgment, procedure admission, filesystem or network access, policy, and explanation remain host-side
  + [decision] FPGA is a backend candidate, not Cantor's source or procedure language

& [InitialConstraintLedger] is active
  + CRTA-C001 preserve every raw source and inherited phase-lock hash
  + CRTA-C002 do not widen CTPR-SJS-01 beyond its explicit authority
  + CRTA-C003 keep runtime, procedure language, and hardware target identities distinct
  + CRTA-C004 require deterministic logical time and normalized replay for the first runtime
  + CRTA-C005 require typed immutable messages instead of shared mutable attention
  + CRTA-C006 make every procedure phase explicit, versioned, attributable, and separately authorized
  + CRTA-C007 fail closed on stale procedure, schema, generation, SOP identity, signature, budget, or compiler identity
  + CRTA-C008 permit no live clock, provider, filesystem, network, Git mutation, database service, notification, model mutation, or external effect in the first proof
  + CRTA-C009 do not call model K/V cache, hidden state, or weights a transportable shared inference state
  + CRTA-C010 require software and future hardware targets to produce the same normalized trace and typed result for the same admitted input
  + CRTA-C011 defer FPGA toolchain and target selection until measured kernel requirements exist
  + CRTA-C012 preserve residual uncertainty and never equate compilation with truth, safety, admission, or correctness

& [SJSWorkOrder] is dependency-aware
  + first explode CTPR Runtime Core and Cantor Process Procedure separately under the shared constraint ledger
  + then refine identities, lifecycle, effects, scheduling, procedure phases, messages, IR, tool boundaries, and proof semantics
  + then design data, transitions, budgets, failure records, and correspondence records
  + then run seven-fold constraints, pruning, opposite-edge review, and dual-hemisphere critique
  + then emit distinct canonicals, registries, matrices, signatures, and phase locks
  + keep FPGA profile unsigned or separately signed as a non-executable target contract unless target-specific evidence reaches satisfaction

& [CurrentDisposition] preserves momentum honestly
  + [source_intake] is complete
  + [authority] is none_for_new_runtime_or_procedure_work
  + [next] emit separate exploded specifications and a shared constraint trace
  + [implementation_result] is none
